.TH "easel histplot" 1 "@EASEL_DATE@" "Easel @EASEL_VERSION@" "Easel Manual"

.SH NAME
easel histplot \- collate a histogram as an xmgrace datafile


.SH SYNOPSIS
.B easel histplot
[\fIoptions\fR]
.I datafile


.SH DESCRIPTION

.PP
.B easel histplot
summarizes numerical data in the input file
.I datafile,
by plotting a histogram, with an optional fitted distribution and/or fixed
distribution, and writing output for these plotted line(s)
in xmgrace XY format.

.PP
This miniapp was developed for plotting empirical versus predicted
bit score distributions for HMMER and Infernal. The various defaults
are set up for our typical score distributions.

.PP
One real-numbered value (one score) is taken from each line of the input file. 
Each line is split into whitespace-delimited fields, and one field
is converted to this score value. By default this is the first field; this can be 
changed by the 
.B \-f 
option.

.PP
Default output containing xy plotting data for the histogram
and optional distribution lines is in xmgrace XY data
format, sent to standard output.
Output may be directed to a file with the
.B \-o
option.

.PP
If 
.I datafile
is \- (a single dash), input lines are read from stdin
instead of opening a file.


.SH OPTIONS

.TP
.B \-b
Parse the
.I datafile
as a binary file consisting of an array of double-precision floats,
rather than parsing it as a text file.
This option allows more space-efficient collection and analysis
of large samples of score distributions.

.TP
.BI \-f  " n"
Read data from whitespace-delimited field 
.I n
on each line, instead of the first field.
Fields are numbered starting from 1.

.TP
.B \-h 
Print brief help; includes version number and summary of
all options, including expert options.

.TP
.BI \-o  " f"
Send output XY file to file
.I <f>
instead of stdout.

.TP
.BI \-w  " x"
Set the width of each histogram bin to
.I x.
The default is 1.0.

.TP
.BI \-\-max " x"
Set the initial upper bound hint on the collected histogram.  You
expect scores to roughly be in the range min..max. However, the
histogram is dynamically resized as needed, so this is only a hint,
and setting
.B \-\-max
has negligible effect, saving some reallocation. The default
is 100.

.TP
.B \-\-min " x"
Set the initial lower bound hint on the collected histogram, and
also determines the boundaries for each histogram bin i. Each
bin i contains scores (min + iw < x <= min + (i+1)w ).
Default is 0.0.


.TP
.B \-\-surv
Instead of a histogram, collect and plot the observed cumulative survival function
(i.e. 1 - CDF), and for additional fitted or fixed distributions, plot these
lines as survival functions P(X <= x) instead of as binned PDFs
P(minb_i < X <= maxb_i).



.SH OPTIONS FOR FITTING A DISTRIBUTION TO THE DATA

These options perform a fit to the observed data, and plot the fitted
line.

.TP
.B \-\-exptail
Fit the high-scoring tail of the data to an exponential, by maximum
likelihood fitting of location mu and slope lambda parameters.
The default tail mass fraction to be fit is 0.01; use
.B \-t
to specify it.

.TP
.B \-\-exptailloc
Fit the high-scoring tail of the data to an exponential with
a fixed slope lambda, fitting only the location parameter mu.
The mu parameter is determined not by maximum likelihood but simply from the minimum
value of the data in the tail. The default tail mass
is 0.01; use
.B \-t
to specify it.
The default slope lambda is 0.693; use
.B \-\-lambda
to specify it.

.TP
.B \-\-gev
Fit the data to a generalized extreme value distribution (GEV),
using maximum likelihood to estimate shape alpha, location mu, and
slope lambda.

.TP
.B \-\-gumbel
Fit the data to a Gumbel extreme value distribution,
using maximum likelihood to estimate location mu and
slope lambda.

.TP
.B \-\-gumloc
Fit the data to a Gumbel distribution with a fixed lambda
parameter, fitting only the location parameter mu by maximum
likelihood.
The default slope lambda is 0.693; use
.B \-\-lambda
to specify it.
        
.TP
.B \-\-normal
Fit the data to a Gaussian normal distribution, using
maximum likelihood to estimate location mu and standard deviation sigma.

.TP
.BI \-\-trunc " x"
With
.BR \-\-gumbel ,
fit the data to a left-truncated Gumbel distribution where the low
scores below a threshold phi = 
.I x
were not observed. (That is, all data points have values >=
.I x.)
The location parameter mu and slope parameter lambda are
fit by maximum likelihood.
The truncation threshold phi should not be much greater than the
location parameter mu (i.e., the bulk of the distribution needs to be
observed), or the fit may be unreliable or even fail to converge,
because for phi >> mu, the tail of the Gumbel converges to a
scale-free exponential and the mu location parameter becomes
impossible to identify.



.SH OPTIONS FOR PLOTTING A FIXED DISTRIBUTION

These options plot a line showing a distribution for comparison, using
specified parameters.

.TP
.B \-\-showgum
Plot a known Gumbel distribution for comparison.
Defaults parameters are location mu = 0, slope lambda = 0.693 (log 2); use
.B \-\-mu,
.B \-\-lambda
to specify them.

.TP
.B \-\-showexp
Plot a known exponential tail for comparison.
Default parameters are tail mass t = 0.01, location mu = 0.0, slope lambda = 0.693; use 
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-\-mu ,
.B \-\-lambda
to specify them.

.TP
.B \-\-showgev
Plot a known generalized extreme value (GEV) distribution for comparison.
Defaults parameters are shape alpha = 0, location mu = 0, slope lambda = 0.693; use
.B \-\-alpha,
.B \-\-mu,
.B \-\-lambda
to specify them.

.TP
.BI \-\-alpha " x"
Specify the alpha shape parameter for the GEV. Default 0.0.

.TP
.BI \-\-lambda " x"
Specify the lambda slope parameter for the Gumbel, GEV, or exponential
tail. Default log 2, 0.693.
This option also controls the lambda parameter for
.BR \-\-gumloc .

.TP
.BI \-\-mu " x"
Specify the mu location parameter for the Gumbel or GEV.
Default 0.0.

.TP
.BI \-t " x"
Specify the high-scoring tail mass fraction for the exponential tail, as a probability. 0 <=
x <= 1. Default 0.01.
This option also controls the mass fraction to be fit by
.B \-\-exptail
and
.BR \-\-exptailloc .


.SH SEE ALSO

.B man easel
for more information about 
Easel miniapps, their shared behaviors, and 
some standardized options.

